# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-08-23)
# On a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` — Git for Windows' own
# default — Git rewrote backend-django/entrypoint.sh with CRLF endings.
# That file is COPY'd into a Linux image and exec'd, so its shebang became
# `#!/bin/sh\r`; the kernel then looked for an interpreter literally named
# `/bin/sh\r`, didn't find one, and the container crash-looped with
#
#   exec ./entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which names the script, not the interpreter, and so reads like a missing
# COPY rather than a line-ending problem. It cost a debugging round-trip.
# Django never started, so nginx in the `frontend` container couldn't
# resolve the `django` upstream either and crash-looped on top of it — one
# root cause, two red containers.
#
# Anything executed inside a container is pinned to LF here regardless of
# the developer's platform or autocrlf setting. Leaving the rest of the
# tree alone is deliberate: `text=auto` for everything would rewrite every
# file in the repo for no benefit, and only these are read by a Linux
# kernel or shell.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.dockerfile text eol=lf

# nginx tolerates CRLF in most directives but not reliably inside
# multi-line blocks, and this one is COPY'd into the frontend image.
*.conf text eol=lf

# docker-compose reads these on the host, but a CRLF value in an
# environment string is carried verbatim into the container (a trailing
# \r in a password or URL is invisible in logs and fails at connect time).
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Go rewrites go.mod/go.sum with LF whenever it touches them, so pinning
# these avoids a spurious whole-file diff every time `go mod tidy` runs on
# a Windows checkout. They were CRLF here; Go parses that fine, but the
# churn is noise.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
